---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Badge from '../components/Badge.astro';
import LicenceNotice from '../components/LicenceNotice.astro';
import { FEEDBACK_URL, LINKS } from '../config';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const sources = (await getCollection('references')).filter((r) => r.data.status === 'real');
---
<Base title="About and methodology" description="What this prototype resource is and how entries are structured." section="about">
  <div class="section-head">
    <h1>About and methodology</h1>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What this resource is</h2>
    <p>
      This prototype illustrates how a continuously updated resource on agentic AI in organisations could be
      structured. It builds on two earlier pieces of work: a paper setting out the <a href={LINKS.phase1Paper}>conceptual
      foundations of agentic AI</a>, and a paper presenting <a href={LINKS.phase2Paper}>early insights from practitioner
      interviews</a>. The challenges and governance-responses categories used here are adapted from the second paper.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="neutral">
    <h2 id="neutral">Neutral and fact-based</h2>
    <p>
      Entries are self-reported by organisations or drawn from interviews. They are not assessed, rated or endorsed by
      GPAI or the OECD. The resource does not score, rank or compare organisations' performance, and lists are not
      ordered by any measure of quality. Use cases do not name the specific models or products used; the focus is on
      use cases, challenges and governance responses.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="fields">
    <h2 id="fields">Common fields and free narrative</h2>
    <p>
      Each use case combines two kinds of information. <strong>Common fields</strong> — such as sector, application
      area, deployment stage, autonomy level, challenges and governance responses — use shared categories so that use
      cases can be filtered and compared. <strong>In their own words</strong> is a free narrative in which the
      organisation describes the use case, what changed and what did not work as expected.
    </p>
    <p>
      The autonomy levels are provisional definitions for this prototype. Where a governance response is linked to a
      challenge, the link is either made in the source paper or marked as a mapping proposed for this prototype.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="current">
    <h2 id="current">How entries stay current</h2>
    <p>
      Organisations can update their own use cases at any time. Entries are also checked periodically, and each one
      shows the date it was last verified and a history of changes. Submissions and updates are reviewed for
      completeness and consistency before publication; they are not assessed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="subset">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2 id="subset">A broader repository, with a subset on OECD.AI</h2>
      <Badge kind="later" />
    </div>
    <p>
      The full repository can be broader; a curated subset could be featured on OECD.AI. Use the view switch at the top
      of each page to see how the subset would look.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="connected">
    <h2 id="connected">Connected OECD work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href={LINKS.haip}>Hiroshima AI Process (HAIP) Reporting Framework</a> — organisations already reporting under
        the framework could link relevant answers to a use case, to avoid duplicate reporting. <Badge kind="later" />
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={LINKS.toolsMetrics}>Catalogue of Tools &amp; Metrics</a> — governance responses that take the form of
        technical or procedural tools could link to the catalogue; see <a href={url('/responses/')}>Governance responses</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href={LINKS.incidentsMonitor}>AI Incidents Monitor</a> — related incident information could in future be
        shown alongside the challenges recorded here. <Badge kind="later" />
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="research-h" id="research">
    <h2 id="research-h">For researchers</h2>
    <p>
      Because every use case uses the same common fields, the resource could serve as a structured dataset on how
      organisations deploy agentic AI, the challenges they encounter and the governance responses they adopt. Links to
      related research on each challenge are to be curated. <Badge kind="later" />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="prototype">
    <h2 id="prototype">About this prototype</h2>
    <p>
      This is a concept prototype for discussion, prepared by the GPAI Tokyo Centre (NICT). It is not an OECD website.
      All organisations and use cases shown are fictional, and their sources and update histories are illustrative.
      The submission form does not send any data.
      {FEEDBACK_URL && <> <a href={FEEDBACK_URL}>Send feedback on this prototype</a>.</>}
    </p>
    <p>Feature badges indicate a possible sequence: <Badge kind="mvp" /> for a first version, <Badge kind="later" /> for possible later additions.</p>
    <h3>Sources</h3>
    <ul>
      {sources.map((s) => (
        <li>
          {s.data.authors}{s.data.year && ` (${s.data.year})`}, {s.data.url ? <a href={s.data.url}>{s.data.title}</a> : s.data.title}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <h3>Licence and adaptation notice</h3>
    <LicenceNotice />
  </section>
</Base>
